Yes, you can absolutely encrypt text with a key and output it as a string.

However, there is a crucial distinction to make before looking at the code:

1.  **Symmetric Encryption (AES):** This is the standard way to encrypt data. It requires a **Key** (a random string) and an **IV** (Initialization Vector). The output is usually binary data (bytes). To make it a string, you must **encode** those bytes (usually using Base64).
2.  **Simple Substitution (Caesar/Vigenère):** This is a "toy" algorithm. It is **not secure** for real-world use, but it is easy to implement in C++ without external libraries.

Below are examples for both.

### Option 1: Secure Encryption (AES-256) using OpenSSL
**Recommended for real applications.**
This uses the industry-standard AES algorithm. The output is Base64 encoded so it can be stored as a string.

*Note: This requires the `libssl` and `libcrypto` libraries to be installed on your system.*

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <openssl/evp.h>
#include <openssl/bio.h>
#include <openssl/err.h>
#include <openssl/rand.h>

// Helper function to convert binary data to Base64 string
std::string bytesToBase64(const unsigned char* data, size_t len) {
    BIO* bio = BIO_new(BIO_f_base64());
    BIO* out = BIO_new(BIO_s_mem());
    BIO_set_flags(bio, BIO_FLAGS_BASE64_NO_NL); // No newlines in output
    BIO_push(bio, out);
    BIO_write(bio, data, len);
    BIO_flush(bio);
    char* buffer = nullptr;
    int len_out = BIO_get_mem_ptr(out, &buffer);
    std::string result(buffer, len_out);
    BIO_free_all(bio);
    return result;
}

// Helper function to convert Base64 string to binary data
std::string base64ToBytes(const std::string& input) {
    BIO* bio = BIO_new_mem_buf(input.c_str(), -1);
    BIO* out = BIO_new(BIO_s_mem());
    BIO_push(bio, out);
    BIO_set_flags(bio, BIO_FLAGS_BASE64_NO_NL);
    BIO_read(bio, nullptr, 0); // Initialize
    unsigned char* buffer = nullptr;
    int len_out = BIO_get_mem_ptr(out, &buffer);
    std::string result(buffer, len_out);
    BIO_free_all(bio);
